; exit 70 (see .exit)
; ABORT TO AN ARENA MARK, for the out-of-memory trap.
;
; `tests/stdlib/314-out-of-memory.ax` says the allocator names the
; failure and exits 70, and that there is no recoverable
; out-of-memory condition. The second half of that is no longer true
; inside a recovery point, and this is where the difference is
; written down: `MM-ALLOC-7`'s status arrives at the arming call
; instead of at the process.
;
; It is also the trap for which recovery is worth the most, and the
; reason is `MM-ALLOC-13`. The abort resets the arena to the mark,
; which puts every chunk mapped since the mark back on
; `@__axiom_free` - so a worker that could not satisfy one request
; hands back the memory that request was building on and is able to
; answer the next one. `usable` after the recovery is the proof that
; it can: an allocation, a store, and a load back, all through the
; arena that just refused a mapping.
;
; THE SIZE IS 2^60 AND THAT IS NOT ARBITRARY - the argument is
; `314-out-of-memory.ax`'s and it transfers unchanged. macOS
; overcommits, so a request for 1 TiB succeeds here and the failure
; path is never reached; the size has to be past the user address
; space on every target this compiler emits for, so that the mapping
; is refused rather than merely unbacked. It was 2^47 until
; 2026-08-29, when FreeBSD 14.4/arm64 GRANTED it - that kernel has 48
; bits of user address space and accounts no overcommit - and this
; case printed the address instead of 70. 2^60 is past all six.
(import IO)

(import Str)

(import Mem)

(:: ask (-> Int Int))

(fn (ask x) (memAlloc 1152921504606846976))

(:: usable (-> Int Int))

(fn (usable x)
  (let ((p (memAlloc 64)))
    {
      (memSetWord p 0 4242)
      (memGetWord p 0)
    }
  )
)

(:: main Int)

;@axiom:effect(io)
(fn (main)
  (let (
    (inside (__axiom_recover
      __axiom_arena_mark      (lambda (x) (ask x))
    ))
    (again (usable 0))
  )
    {
      (println "recovered {inside}")
      (println "usable {again}")
      ; Outside every recovery point: 70, on fd 2, and the program
      ; stops here.
      (ask 0)
      ; Spelled `printlnLit` and not `println` for the reason
      ; `314-out-of-memory.ax` spells its own unreachable line that
      ; way: `verify-stdlib-out.py` MODELS `(println "literal")` as a
      ; claim that the literal appears in the golden, and this line is
      ; deliberately never printed. It is still a tripwire - if the
      ; trap outside a recovery point ever stops stopping the program,
      ; stdout gains a line and the golden says so.
      (printlnLit (strCStr "UNREACHABLE: the trap outside a recovery point returned"))
    }
  )
)
